<p>The United States plans to offer on-site passport and related consular services in the Israeli settlement of Efrat in the occupied West Bank, with services set to begin February 27, according to reporting that cited US officials. Reports describe the move as a way to make documentation easier for US citizens living in the area, including tens of thousands of American-Israelis in the West Bank.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/xko2fdrdGO0IBEphf7kZcDUhOl8mk-evEFq5jX_5hlRunQPXdtmyjkbaPhjem9Nggr1LvDdFtq8ePWfZ0Mud4qsGJHTMQFi7rQ1KmFe4a-fGYH8PFFN6Ju7-Wr_NYybzkBEyrFj87nlbH2Ig0kcT5nFgVe2Eu09R7fU3ppQtqMB-takwo6BjSHKFetofMp4K62t8mGoeVUuLXQBdJaEgi4PSayFcDAOPacJDd7Ct1qRKvV2JySasuM_8QMt6yC16FH-80M0VNX81xLkx-jMKUS70Ug' alt='An Israeli settlement in the occupied West Bank, as US officials announce on-site passport services.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Scale of potential users: One report says tens of thousands of American-Israelis live in the West Bank, suggesting a sizable pool of potential applicants.</li><li>How it will be delivered: US officials said consular staff will provide passport services on-site “this week,” rather than through routine in-office processing.</li><li>Settlement politics backdrop: Reporting notes that many on Israel’s right support annexing the West Bank, a backdrop for settlement-related moves.</li><li>Precedent: US officials said this will be the first time American consular staff provide on-site services in a West Bank settlement.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>US officials: US officials said American consular staff will provide on-site passport services in a West Bank settlement for the first time. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/us-offer-passport-services-west-bank-settlement-first-time'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Israel (government position): Israel disputes that West Bank settlements are illegal under international law, according to reporting summarizing Israel’s stance. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.